---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import figure from '../assets/chapters/collaborative-modeling-domain.png';

const description =
  'Learn about collaborative modeling, a visualization technique for analyzing '
  + 'complex decision-making processes with stakeholders. Understand how to use '
  + 'domain modeling to create shared understanding in software development. '
  + 'Explore more in Chapter 2 of our book.';

/* The live page gives this figure an empty alt. It is a diagram of the six
   stages, which is an idea rather than a decoration, so it gets a real one. */
const figureAlt =
  'The six stages of collaborative modeling: prep, then a session that runs '
  + 'check-in, modeling with tools — diverging then converging — check-out and '
  + 'a retrospective, ending in communicating and documentation.';
---
<ChapterLayout number="02" title="What is collaborative modeling?" description={description}
               figure={figure} figureAlt={figureAlt} figurePlacement="full">
  <p slot="lede">
    Collaborative modeling is “a visualization technique to analyze complex and
    conflict-laden decision-making processes with all relevant stakeholders and
    decision-makers to create a shared understanding.” It is not innate to software
    development and is sometimes referred to as participatory modeling.
  </p>

  <p>
    When exploring business problems through collaborative modeling, it is important
    that the focus is on discovering the domain, not the specific collaborative
    modeling tool being used. In collaborative modeling, “domain” refers to the
    specific area of knowledge or expertise being explored or modeled. The domain
    represents the subject matter or part of the organization that we, together with
    the stakeholders, are trying to understand and represent through collaborative
    modeling. Using collaborative modeling to understand the domain is also called
    domain modeling. Read more in Chapter 2 of our book to understand more about
    collaborative modelling and the tools you can use.
  </p>
</ChapterLayout>
